Q: Pseudocode / flowchart help needed! (Python Coding Class) A: <p>Two inputs with the same prompt should tell you that this design isn't great. Also, it's strictly limited to dog &amp; cat. How would you extend this code to handle more than 2 animals?</p>
<p>The following approach is more flexible:</p>
<pre><code># a dictionary of animal types and the sounds they make
ANIMALS = {&quot;cat&quot;: &quot;meow&quot;, &quot;dog&quot;: &quot;woof&quot;}

def emit_sound(_sound: str, _name: str) -&gt; None:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    print _sound once for each character in _name
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    print(&quot; &quot;.join([_sound] * len(_name)))


# ideally this should be cached
def prompt() -&gt; str:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    This code ensures that the input prompt makes sense
    when there are more than 2 items in the ANIMALS dictionary
    Try adding a new item to see how this affects the input prompt
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    assert len(ANIMALS) &gt; 1
    at = list(ANIMALS)
    atp = &quot;, &quot;.join(at[:-1]) + f&quot; or {at[-1]}&quot;
    return f&quot;Do you have a {atp}? &quot;

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    # empty input will terminate the loop
    while animal := input(prompt()):
        if (sound := ANIMALS.get(animal)) is None:
            print(f&quot;I don't know about {animal}&quot;)
        else:
            name = input(f&quot;What is your {animal}'s name? &quot;)
            # an empty line will be emitted if `name` is empty
            # could add an additional check here
            emit_sound(sound, name)
</code></pre>
